Sure! Here's a simple HTML website that displays a random joke from a list of 4 jokes when a user clicks a button:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Random Joke Generator</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
            margin-top: 50px;
            background-color: #f0f8ff;
        }
        button {
            padding: 10px 20px;
            font-size: 16px;
            cursor: pointer;
            background-color: #4CAF50;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 5px;
        }
        button:hover {
            background-color: #45a049;
        }
        #joke {
            margin-top: 20px;
            font-size: 18px;
            color: #333;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>Random Joke Generator</h1>
    <button onclick="showJoke()">Click for a Joke</button>
    <div id="joke"></div>

    <script>
        const jokes = [
            "Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field!",
            "What do you call a fake noodle? An impasta!",
            "Why did the math book look sad? Because it had too many problems.",
            "What do you get when you cross a snowman and a vampire? Frostbite!"
        ];

        function showJoke() {
            const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
            document.getElementById("joke").textContent = jokes[randomIndex];
        }
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works:
- The HTML defines a simple layout with a button and a place to show the joke.
- The CSS styles the page for a clean look.
- The JavaScript defines an array of 4 jokes and selects a random one when the button is clicked.

You can copy and paste this code into an `.html` file and open it in a web browser to try it out.

